and orientation\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4941b18\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3223\nmechanism=0.4330\nlexical=0.0193\ncode_feature=0.2000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, object:path, object:rectangle, operation:recolor, operation:sort_order, output:same_size, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e4941b18.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e4941b18.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove red into teal's top marker position, then move teal to the bottom outside corner of the grey rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds one filled rectangular non-background block.\n- There is exactly one red marker and one teal marker, both in the row immediately above the rectangle.\n- Both markers are horizontally aligned over columns inside the rectangle.\n\nRule\n- Clear the original red marker.\n- Recolor the original teal marker cell red.\n- Place the teal marker beside the rectangle's bottom row, on the side indicated by the horizontal direction from red to teal.\n\nKey insight\n- The red-to-teal horizontal order chooses the rectangle side where teal exits at the bottom.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"teal marker s above a filled rectangle, with a red marker on the same row\",\n  \"path\": \"from s toward the rectangle side away from the red marker, then down to the background cell beside the rectangle's bottom row\",\n  \"dst\": \"place s at that bottom-side endpoint\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"paint only the endpoint teal\",\n  \"transform\": \"clear the original red marker to background; recolor s.old cell red; keep the rectangle and other background unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9b5080bb\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2967\nmechanism=0.3263\nlexical=0.0142\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:rectangle, operation:recolor, output:same_size, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9b5080bb.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/9b5080bb.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecolor the small defect bars on each shared-color object with the opposite panel color.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains two large panel colors and one shared object color.\n- Each shared-color object is a mostly solid rectangle inside exactly one panel.\nRule\n- Find the near-solid rectangular core of each object.\n- Missing core pixels and one-cell protrusions define two-pixel bars perpendicular to the nearest core edge.\n- Recolor those bars with the other panel color; leave all other pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\n- The colors to add are not tied to numeric ids: each object's surrounding panel identifies the opposite panel color.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"shared-color near-rectangular objects embedded in exactly one of two panel colors\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each object, find its near-solid rectangular core; recolor two-pixel bars from missing core-edge pixels or one-cell protrusions to the opposite panel color; leave all other pixels unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"34cfa167\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2927\nmechanism=0.3560\nlexical=0.0134\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:rectangle, operation:sort_order, output:same_size, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/34cfa167.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/34cfa167.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nComplete a four-corner framed rectangle from one decorated anchor rectangle and its diagonal mate.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Two same-color solid rectangular anchors of the same size sit on opposite\n  diagonal corners, with the first anchor above-left of the second.\n- The upper-left anchor has two one-cell-thick vertical control bars to its\n  right and two one-cell-thick horizontal control bars below it.\n\nRule:\n- Copy the anchor rectangle into the two missing corners of the implied\n  larger rectangle.\n- Repeat the right-side control bars horizontally between the left and right\n  anchors, using their spacing as the period, and draw the outer top and\n  bottom borders in the first right-control color.\n- Repeat the below-side control bars vertically between the top and bottom\n  anchors, using the same period, and draw the outer left and right borders\n  in the first below-control color.\n\nKey insight: The small control bars are the first two ticks of two periodic\nframe patterns; the diagonal anchor supplies the opposite corner and the\nmissing two anchor copies.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"two same-color solid rectangular anchors, with two right control bars and two below control bars beside the upper-left anchor\",\n  \"dst\": \"the missing two anchor corners and periodic frame segments between all four anchors\",\n  \"transform\": \"copy the anchor rectangle into the missing corners; repeat the right-control colors across the top and bottom anchor rows and outer borders, and repeat the below-control colors down the left and right side bars using the same spacing\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"221dfab4\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3139\nmechanism=0.3973\nlexical=0.0188\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:path, operation:flood_fill, operation:recolor, operation:sort_order, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=